          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            the kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            corresponding in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+object"} quotient object
            \/
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#from_higher_inductive_types From higher inductive types
          %p
            Quotient type may be constructed as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "higher+inductive+type#QuotientsOfSets"} here
          %h4#history_and_effectiveness History and effectiveness
          %p
            This notion of quotient type predates the general notion of higher inductive type, however; it appears already in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HofmannThesis"} Martin Hofmann’s thesis
          %p
            Hofmann works in a theory that includes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+propositions"} type of propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prop
            that may be assumed to satisfy
            %span.newWikiWord
              proof irrelevance
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/proof+irrelevance"} ?
            — for instance, it might be the universe of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-propositions"} h-propositions
            — and considers the
            %em effectiveness
            of quotient types only for quotients of Prop-valued
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relations"} equivalence relations
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr-exact+category"} Barr-exact category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+extensionality"} propositional extensionality
            (which in turn follows from the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
          %p
            However, before the introduction of homotopy type theory and the realization of the importance of homotopy levels such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-propositions"} h-propositions
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoids"} setoids
            %a{:href => "#Maietti99"} Maietti
            shows, by adapting
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle#RelationToTheAxiomOfChoice"} Diaconescu’s argument
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %h3#FromUnivalence From univalence
          %p
            Quotient types are implied by adding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
          %p A sufficient set of hypothesis that imply the existence of quotient types is this:
          %ul
            %li
              ambient
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+types"} dependent sum types
              and
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            %li
              availability of
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncation"} propositional truncation
            %li
              existence of a univalent family
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %li
              every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-prop"} h-prop
              is equivalent to some type in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            In terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+categories"} fibration categories
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+categories"} fibration categories
            where every map is a fibration, “hprop” means “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"}> subobject
            ”, propositional truncation becomes a stable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            factorisation, and from a univalent universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            containing all hprops, we can construct a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi U
                  %mi isProp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{x \colon U}{\sum} isProp(x)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (For the subobject classification axioms: existence comes from “every hprop is equivalent to some type in
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            So in this setting, the HoTT argument just becomes the usual construction (due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Pare"} Paré 74
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#MacLaneMoerdijk92"} MacLane-Moerdijk 92, IV.5
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "quotient+object#InToposes"} quotient object – in toposes
          %p
            One thing to be careful about, in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+category"} fibration category
            setting, is that “equivalences” as defined in type theory correspond not generally to the original equivalences, but to the right homotopy equivalences, which of course may be different. This is why e.g. Mike Shulman’s def of “type-theoretic fibration category” doesn’t include a class of equivalences as separate data, but takes them to be the right homot equivs. So for the type-theoretic arguments to apply, “univalent fibration” should be defined using right homot equivs.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoid"}> setoid
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop+set"} Bishop set
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+stack"} quotient stack
          %h2#references References
          %p
            On the convoluted history of the notion of quotient types, from
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoids"} setoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop+sets"} Bishop sets
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            and in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+foundations+of+mathematics"} univalent foundations of mathematics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Nuo Li,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quotient Types in Type Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://eprints.nottingham.ac.uk/28941/1/Nuo%20Li%27s_Thesis.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://core.ac.uk/display/33573841"} oai:eprints.nottingham.ac.uk:28941
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Li-QuotientTypes.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Murray22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zachary+Murray"}> Zachary Murray
                , §4.3.2 in:
                %em Constructive Analysis in the Agda Proof Assistant
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.08354"}> arXiv:2205.08354
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/z-murray/honours-project-constructive-analysis-in-agda"} github
          %h3#as_a_higher_inductive_type As a higher inductive type
          %p
            As
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
          %ul
            %li#HofmannThesis
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hofmann"}> Martin Hofmann
                , p. 111 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Extensional concepts in intensional type theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.lfcs.inf.ed.ac.uk/reports/95/ECS-LFCS-95-327/"} ECS-LFCS-95-327
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/HofmannExtensionalIntensionalTypeTheory.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4471-0963-1"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4471-0963-1
            %li#Maietti99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maria+Emilia+Maietti"}> Maria Emilia Maietti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em About effective quotients in constructive type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thorsten+Altenkirch"}> Thorsten Altenkirch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Capriotti"} Paolo Capriotti
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabe+Dijkstra"} Gabe Dijkstra
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolai+Kraus"} Nicolai Kraus
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredrik+Forsberg"} Fredrik Forsberg
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quotient inductive-inductive types
                %strong 10803
                (2018) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-89366-2_16"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-319-89366-2_16
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.02346"} arXiv:1612.02346
          %h3#FromUnivalenceReferences From univalence
          %p
            The argument that adding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            (plus universe resizing) to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            implies the existence of quotient types is attributed to Voevodsky in places like here:
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://benediktahrens.net/talks/IRIT_slides.pdf"} pdf, slides 41 &amp; 61
          %p Voevodsky’s own account of this seems to be entirely in his Coq-code repository here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Equivalence classes with respect to a given relation
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://github.com/vladimirias/Foundations/blob/df19211f602ab71ee44f0ae4de2a19170c5b9e4d/hlevel2/hSet.v#L466"}> here
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Set quotients of types
                %a{:href => "https://github.com/vladimirias/Foundations/blob/df19211f602ab71ee44f0ae4de2a19170c5b9e4d/hlevel2/hSet.v#L555"} here
          %p A more readable version of this is in section 6.10 of
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+Theory+--+Univalent+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
          %p Def. 6.10.5 there is the definition of the quotients. This uses truncation, for which something like univalence and resizing is necessary and univalence and truncation is sufficient. Then theorem 6.10.6 right below that definition checks that these quotient do indeed behave like quotients should, and this is where univalence proper comes in.
          %p This discussion of quotients from univalence in the HoTT book originally comes from
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Egbert+Rijke"}> Egbert Rijke
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bas+Spitters"} Bas Spitters
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sets in homotopy type theory
              %strong 25
              5 “From type theory and homotopy theory to Univalent Foundations of Mathematics” (2015) 1172-1202
              %p
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129514000553"}> doi:10.1017/S0960129514000553
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1305.3835"} arXiv:1305.3835
          %p The relevant section there is 2.4 “Voevodsky’s impredicative quotients”, see def. 2.24 and the lines right below.
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-version" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Pare74
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"}> Robert Paré
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Colimits in Topoi
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/journals/bull/1974-80-03/S0002-9904-1974-13497-X/S0002-9904-1974-13497-X.pdf"} pdf
          %p reviewed for instance in
          %ul
            %li#MacLaneMoerdijk92
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
                  – A first introduction to topos theory
          %p A more elementary discussion is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"}> Todd Trimble
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/toddtrimble/published/An+elementary+approach+to+elementary+topos+theory"} An elementary approach to elementary topos theory
        .revisedby
